Imagine a guessing game where one participant selects a set of images and another selects candidates one at a time. A correct selection adds progress. An incorrect selection ends the attempt. A round finishes only when every selected image has been found.
This article examines a local R45 snapshot of an application I have been building with AI assistance. It focuses on the API contract and state transitions, without claiming that a source review establishes complete concurrency safety. The article was prepared with AI from project records and that source snapshot; the full release test suite was not rerun for this article.
The controller accepts the following Zod schema:
export const answerSchema = z.object({
  attemptGroupId: z.string().uuid(),
  selectedImageIds: z.array(z.string().uuid()).length(1),
}).strict();

The array contains exactly one image identifier. The client reports its selection; it does not supply a trusted verdict. Fields such as correct, result, and viaKey are outside this contract. The associated test file includes rejection cases for those extra fields.
Input shape validation is only the first stage. The server also checks whether the selected image belongs to the current candidate set before comparing it with the stored correct answers. A well-formed identifier is not necessarily a valid move.
The completion condition in the reviewed handler is:
const groupCompleted =
  isCorrect && sameSet(nextProgress, group.correct_image_ids);

The helper removes duplicates, sorts the arrays, and compares their lengths and elements. For a hypothetical correct set of A and B, choosing A is progress, not completion. Choosing B afterwards completes the round. Reversing the order should lead to the same set.
